  <pre style="white-space: pre-wrap; " class="mb-0 text-dark"><p>what is !== in javascript</p></pre>
</div>

  <div class="google-auto-placed" style="width: 100%; height: auto; clear: both; text-align: center;"><ins data-ad-format="auto" class="adsbygoogle adsbygoogle-noablate" data-ad-client="ca-pub-4747441021236672" data-adsbygoogle-status="done" style="display: block; margin: 10px auto 10px 0px; background-color: transparent; transition: none; outline: none; border: none; padding: 0px; border-spacing: 0px; width: 214px; z-index: 30; height: 214px;" data-ad-status="filled"><div id="aswift_7_host" style="border: none; height: 214px; width: 214px; margin: 0px; padding: 0px; position: relative; visibility: visible; background-color: transparent; display: inline-block; overflow: visible;"><iframe id="aswift_7" name="aswift_7" browsingtopics="true" style="left:0;position:absolute;top:0;border:0;width:214px;height:214px;min-height:auto;max-height:none;min-width:auto;max-width:none;" sandbox="allow-forms allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox allow-same-origin allow-scripts allow-top-navigation-by-user-activation" width="214" height="214" frameborder="0" marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" vspace="0" hspace="0" allowtransparency="true" scrolling="no" allow="attribution-reporting; run-ad-auction" src="cid:frame-5B6947E778EF500C1559882029CEE7B6@mhtml.blink" data-google-container-id="a!8" tabindex="0" title="Advertisement" aria-label="Advertisement" data-google-query-id="CIqb3av6440DFbHqGAIdqnwBLg" data-load-complete="true"></iframe></div></ins></div><div class="bg-secondary" style="height: auto !important;">
 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:637357,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-637357&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003e!==\u003c/code\u003e operator in JavaScript is called the \&quot;strict inequality\&quot; operator. It checks if two values are not equal and also checks their types. If the values are not equal or their types are different, it will return \u003ccode\u003etrue\u003c/code\u003e. Otherwise, it will return \u003ccode\u003efalse\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere's an example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-637357-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-637357-0\&quot;\u003elet a = 5;\nlet b = '5';\n\nconsole.log(a !== b); // This will print: true, because 5 (number) and '5' (string) are of different types\nconsole.log(a !== 5); // This will print: false, because both are equal and of the same type\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor more details, you can check the documentation here: \u003ca href=\&quot;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Strict_inequality\&quot; target=\&quot;_blank\&quot;\u003eMDN Web Docs - Strict Equality\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/637357/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/637357/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;The `!==` operator in JavaScript is called the \&quot;strict inequality\&quot; operator. It checks if two values are not equal and also checks their types. If the values are not equal or their types are different, it will return `true`. Otherwise, it will return `false`.\n\nHere's an example:\n\n```javascript\nlet a = 5;\nlet b = '5';\n\nconsole.log(a !== b); // This will print: true, because 5 (number) and '5' (string) are of different types\nconsole.log(a !== 5); // This will print: false, because both are equal and of the same type\n```\n\nFor more details, you can check the documentation here: [MDN Web Docs - Strict Equality](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Strict_inequality)&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;what is !== in javascript&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:6.38,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0" style="height: auto !important;"><div style="height: auto !important;"><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer" style="height: auto !important;"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 6.38 seconds</div></div></div><div style="height: auto !important;"><p>The <code>!==</code> operator in JavaScript is called the "strict inequality" operator. It checks if two values are not equal and also checks their types. If the values are not equal or their types are different, it will return <code>true</code>. Otherwise, it will return <code>false</code>.</p><div class="google-auto-placed ap_container" style="width: 100%; height: auto; clear: both; text-align: center;"><ins data-ad-format="auto" class="adsbygoogle adsbygoogle-noablate" data-ad-client="ca-pub-4747441021236672" data-adsbygoogle-status="done" style="display: block; margin: auto auto auto -30px; background-color: t