 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:634824,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-634824&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eIn JavaScript, the \u003ccode\u003e!==\u003c/code\u003e operator is known as the \&quot;strict not equal\&quot; operator. It is used to compare two values and check if they are not equal in both value and type. If the values are different in type or value, it returns \u003ccode\u003etrue\u003c/code\u003e; otherwise, it returns \u003ccode\u003efalse\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere’s a simple example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-634824-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-634824-0\&quot;\u003elet a = '5';\nlet b = 5;\n\nconsole.log(a !== b); // true because one is a string and the other is a number\nconsole.log(b !== 5); // false because they are the same in both value and type\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can find more details about operators in JavaScript \u003ca href=\&quot;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators\&quot; target=\&quot;_blank\&quot;\u003ehere\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/634824/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/634824/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;In JavaScript, the `!==` operator is known as the \&quot;strict not equal\&quot; operator. It is used to compare two values and check if they are not equal in both value and type. If the values are different in type or value, it returns `true`; otherwise, it returns `false`.\n\nHere’s a simple example:\n\n```javascript\nlet a = '5';\nlet b = 5;\n\nconsole.log(a !== b); // true because one is a string and the other is a number\nconsole.log(b !== 5); // false because they are the same in both value and type\n```\n\nYou can find more details about operators in JavaScript [here](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators).&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;meaning of !== in java script&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:5.41,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0" style="height: auto !important;"><div style="height: auto !important;"><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer" style="height: auto !important;"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 5.41 seconds</div></div></div><div style="height: auto !important;"><p>In JavaScript, the <code>!==</code> operator is known as the "strict not equal" operator. It is used to compare two values and check if they are not equal in both value and type. If the values are different in type or value, it returns <code>true</code>; otherwise, it returns <code>false</code>.</p><div class="google-auto-placed ap_container" style="width: 100%; height: auto; clear: both; text-align: center;"><ins data-ad-format="auto" class="adsbygoogle adsbygoogle-noablate" data-ad-client="ca-pub-4747441021236672" data-adsbygoogle-status="done" style="display: block; margin: auto auto auto -30px; background-color: transparent; transition: none; outline: none; border: none; padding: 0px; border-spacing: 0px; width: 214px; z-index: 30; height: 214px;" d