 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:628731,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-628731&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eTo loop through an array and an object until a maximum number of iterations (or questions) has been reached, you can use a for loop. Here’s an example in JavaScript:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-628731-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-628731-0\&quot;\u003econst array = ['Question 1', 'Question 2', 'Question 3'];\nconst obj = { a: 'Question 4', b: 'Question 5' };\nconst maxQuestions = 5; // Set your maximum number of questions\nlet count = 0;\n\n// Loop through the array\nfor (let i = 0; i \u0026lt; array.length \u0026amp;\u0026amp; count \u0026lt; maxQuestions; i++) {\n    console.log(array[i]);\n    count++;\n}\n\n// Loop through the object\nfor (const key in obj) {\n    if (count \u0026gt;= maxQuestions) break; // Check if maxQuestions is reached\n    console.log(obj[key]);\n    count++;\n}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this code:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWe have an array and an object, each containing some questions.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWe set a maximum number of questions to be displayed.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWe first loop through the array and print each question until we reach the maximum.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eThen, we loop through the object in a similar way, stopping if we've reached the max.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can modify the \u003ccode\u003emaxQuestions\u003c/code\u003e variable