  <div class="bg-secondary">
 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:626728,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-626728&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003e.map\u003c/code\u003e function is a built-in method in JavaScript that creates a new array by applying a function to each element of an existing array. It’s useful for transforming data in an array without changing the original array.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere’s a simple example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-626728-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-626728-0\&quot;\u003econst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];\nconst doubled = numbers.map(number =\u0026gt; number * 2);\n\nconsole.log(doubled); // Output: [2, 4, 6, 8]\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this example, we have an array called \u003ccode\u003enumbers\u003c/code\u003e. We use \u003ccode\u003e.map()\u003c/code\u003e to create a new array called \u003ccode\u003edoubled\u003c/code\u003e where each number is multiplied by 2. The original \u003ccode\u003enumbers\u003c/code\u003e array remains unchanged.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can learn more about the \u003ccode\u003e.map\u003c/code\u003e function on \u003ca href=\&quot;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/map\&quot; target=\&quot;_blank\&quot;\u003eMDN Web Docs\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/626728/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/626728/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;The `.map` function is a built-in method in JavaScript that creates a new array by applying a function to each element of an existing array. It’s useful for transforming data in an array without changing the original array.\n\nHere’s a simple example:\n\n```javascript\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];\nconst doubled = numbers.map(number =\u003e number * 2);\n\nconsole.log(doubled); // Output: [2, 4, 6, 8]\n```\n\nIn this example, we have an array called `numbers`. We use `.map()` to create a new array called `doubled` where each number is multiplied by 2. The original `numbers` array remains unchanged.\n\nYou can learn more about the `.map` function on [MDN Web Docs](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/map).&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;can you explain to me the .map function please\r\n&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:4.1,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 4.1 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>The <code>.map</code> function is a built-in method in JavaScript that creates a new array by applying a function to each element of an existing array. It’s useful for transforming data in an array without changing the original array.</p>

<p>Here’s a simple example:</p>

<pre><header><div>javascript</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-626728-0">Copy code</div>
</div></header><code class="javascript hljs language-javascript" id="code-626728-0" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> numbers = [<span class="hljs-number">1</span>, <span class="hljs-number">2</span>, <span class="hljs-number">3</span>, <span class="hljs-number">4</span>];
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> doubled = numbers.<span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">number</span> =&gt;</span> number * <span class="hljs-number">2</span>);

<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(doubled); <span class="hljs-comment">// Output: [2, 4, 6, 8]</span>
</code></pre>

<p>In this example, we have an array called <code>numbers</code>. We use <code>.map()</code> to create a new array called <code>doubled</code> where each number is multiplied by 2. The original <code>numbers</code> array remains unchanged.</p>

<p>You can learn more about the <code>.map</code> function on <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/map" target="_blank">MDN Web Docs</a>.</p>
</div></div></div></div>
