  <pre style="white-space: pre-wrap; " class="mb-0 text-dark"><p>what are objects in JavaScript</p></pre>
</div>

  <div class="bg-secondary">
 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:626370,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-626370&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eIn JavaScript, objects are collections of key-value pairs. They allow you to group related data and functions together. Each key is a string (also known as a property name), and the value can be anything, including other objects, arrays, or functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere’s a simple example of an object in JavaScript:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-626370-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-626370-0\&quot;\u003econst student = {\n    name: \&quot;John\&quot;,\n    age: 20,\n    isEnrolled: true,\n    greet: function() {\n        console.log(\&quot;Hello, my name is \&quot; + this.name);\n    }\n};\n\n// Accessing properties\nconsole.log(student.name); // Output: John\nconsole.log(student.age); // Output: 20\n\n// Calling a method\nstudent.greet(); // Output: Hello, my name is John\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this example, \u003ccode\u003estudent\u003c/code\u003e is an object with properties like \u003ccode\u003ename\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eage\u003c/code\u003e, and \u003ccode\u003eisEnrolled\u003c/code\u003e, as well as a method called \u003ccode\u003egreet\u003c/code\u003e. You can access the properties using dot notation (like \u003ccode\u003estudent.name\u003c/code\u003e). Objects are really useful for organizing and managing data in your programs!\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/626370/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/626370/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;In JavaScript, objects are collections of key-value pairs. They allow you to group related data and functions together. Each key is a string (also known as a property name), and the value can be anything, including other objects, arrays, or functions.\n\nHere’s a simple example of an object in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nconst student = {\n    name: \&quot;John\&quot;,\n    age: 20,\n    isEnrolled: true,\n    greet: function() {\n        console.log(\&quot;Hello, my name is \&quot; + this.name);\n    }\n};\n\n// Accessing properties\nconsole.log(student.name); // Output: John\nconsole.log(student.age); // Output: 20\n\n// Calling a method\nstudent.greet(); // Output: Hello, my name is John\n```\n\nIn this example, `student` is an object with properties like `name`, `age`, and `isEnrolled`, as well as a method called `greet`. You can access the properties using dot notation (like `student.name`). Objects are really useful for organizing and managing data in your programs!&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;what are objects in JavaScript\r\n&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:3.34,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 3.34 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>In JavaScript, objects are collections of key-value pairs. They allow you to group related data and functions together. Each key is a string (also known as a property name), and the value can be anything, including other objects, arrays, or functions.</p>

<p>Here’s a simple example of an object in JavaScript:</p>

<pre><header><div>javascript</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-626370-0">Copy code</div>
</div></header><code class="javascript hljs language-javascript" id="code-626370-0" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> student = {
    <span class="hljs-attr">name</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"John"</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">age</span>: <span class="hljs-number">20</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">isEnrolled</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">greet</span>: <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
        <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"Hello, my name is "</span> + <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">name</span>);
    }
};

<span class="hljs-comment">// Accessing properties</span>
<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(student.<span class="hljs-property">name</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// Output: John</span>
<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(student.<span class="hljs-property">age</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// Output: 20</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Calling a method</span>
student.<span class="hljs-title function_">greet</span>(); <span class="hljs-comment">// Output: Hello, my name is John</span>
</code></pre>

<p>In this example, <code>student</code> is an object with properties like <code>name</code>, <code>age</code>, and <code>isEnrolled</code>, as well as a method called <code>greet</code>. You can access the properties using dot notation (like <code>student.name</code>). Objects are really useful for organizing and managing data in your programs!</p>
</div></div></div></div>
