 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:626053,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-626053&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eIn JavaScript, \u003ccode\u003eelse if\u003c/code\u003e is used to check multiple conditions in an if statement. It allows you to execute different blocks of code based on different conditions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere's a simple example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-626053-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-626053-0\&quot;\u003elet score = 85;\n\nif (score \u0026gt;= 90) {\n    console.log(\&quot;You got an A!\&quot;);\n} else if (score \u0026gt;= 80) {\n    console.log(\&quot;You got a B!\&quot;);\n} else if (score \u0026gt;= 70) {\n    console.log(\&quot;You got a C!\&quot;);\n} else {\n    console.log(\&quot;You need to study more!\&quot;);\n}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this example, the program checks the \u003ccode\u003escore\u003c/code\u003e variable and prints out a message depending on its value. You can use as many \u003ccode\u003eelse if\u003c/code\u003e statements as you need to check for different conditions.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/626053/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/626053/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;In JavaScript, `else if` is used to check multiple conditions in an if statement. It allows you to execute different blocks of code based on different conditions.\n\nHere's a simple example:\n\n```javascript\nlet score = 85;\n\nif (score \u003e= 90) {\n    console.log(\&quot;You got an A!\&quot;);\n} else if (score \u003e= 80) {\n    console.log(\&quot;You got a B!\&quot;);\n} else if (score \u003e= 70) {\n    console.log(\&quot;You got a C!\&quot;);\n} else {\n    console.log(\&quot;You need to study more!\&quot;);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the program checks the `score` variable and prints out a message depending on its value. You can use as many `else if` statements as you need to check for different conditions.&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;else if in java script&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:2.85,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 2.85 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>In JavaScript, <code>else if</code> is used to check multiple conditions in an if statement. It allows you to execute different blocks of code based on different conditions.</p>

