= \&quot;\u0026lt;p\u0026gt;This is new content!\u0026lt;/p\u0026gt;\&quot;;\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003ch3\u003eExample:\u003c/h3\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere’s a simple example that shows both getting and setting \u003ccode\u003einnerHTML\u003c/code\u003e:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ehtml\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-624975-2\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;html\&quot; id=\&quot;code-624975-2\&quot;\u003e\u0026lt;!DOCTYPE html\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;html lang=\&quot;en\&quot;\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;body\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;div id=\&quot;myDiv\&quot;\u0026gt;Original Content\u0026lt;/div\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;button onclick=\&quot;changeContent()\&quot;\u0026gt;Change Content\u0026lt;/button\u0026gt;\n\n  \u0026lt;script\u0026gt;\n    function changeContent() {\n      // Get the original content\n      let originalContent = document.getElementById(\&quot;myDiv\&quot;).innerHTML;\n      console.log(\&quot;Original Content:\&quot;, originalContent);\n\n      // Set new content\n      document.getElementById(\&quot;myDiv\&quot;).innerHTML = \&quot;\u0026lt;p\u0026gt;This is new content!\u0026lt;/p\u0026gt;\&quot;;\n    }\n  \u0026lt;/script\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;/body\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;/html\u0026gt;\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this example, when you click the button, it will log the original content and change it to new content.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624975/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624975/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;The `.innerHTML` property in JavaScript is used to get or set the HTML content inside an element. Here’s how you can use it:\n\n### To Get HTML Content:\nYou can use `.innerHTML` to retrieve the HTML inside an element. For example:\n\n```javascript\n// Assuming there's a div with the id \&quot;myDiv\&quot;\nlet content = document.getElementById(\&quot;myDiv\&quot;).innerHTML;\nconsole.log(content); // This will log the HTML inside the div\n```\n\n### To Set HTML Content:\nYou can also use `.innerHTML` to change the HTML inside an element. For example:\n\n```javascript\n// Assuming there's a div with the id \&quot;myDiv\&quot;\ndocument.getElementById(\&quot;myDiv\&quot;).innerHTML = \&quot;\u003cp\u003eThis is new content!\u003c/p\u003e\&quot;;\n```\n\n### Example:\nHere’s a simple example that shows both getting and setting `innerHTML`:\n\n```html\n\u003c!DOCTYPE html\u003e\n\u003chtml lang=\&quot;en\&quot;\u003e\n\u003cbody\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\&quot;myDiv\&quot;\u003eOriginal Content\u003c/div\u003e\n  \u003cbutton onclick=\&quot;changeContent()\&quot;\u003eChange Content\u003c/button\u003e\n\n  \u003cscript\u003e\n    function changeContent() {\n      // Get the original content\n      let originalContent = document.getElementById(\&quot;myDiv\&quot;).innerHTML;\n      console.log(\&quot;Original Content:\&quot;, originalContent);\n      \n      // Set new content\n      document.getElementById(\&quot;myDiv\&quot;).innerHTML = \&quot;\u003cp\u003eThis is new content!\u003c/p\u003e\&quot;;\n    }\n  \u003c/script\u003e\n\u003c/body\u003e\n\u003c/html\u003e\n```\n\nIn this example, when you click the button, it will log the original content and change it to new content.&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;how to use \&quot;.innerhtml\&quot;&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:4.94,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 4.94 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>The <code>.innerHTML</code> property in JavaScript is used to get or set the HTML content inside an element. Here’s how you can use it:</p>

