 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:624860,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-624860&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eIn JavaScript, the greater sign \u003ccode\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e is used as a comparison operator to check if the value on the left side is greater than the value on the right side. It returns \u003ccode\u003etrue\u003c/code\u003e if the left value is greater, and \u003ccode\u003efalse\u003c/code\u003e otherwise.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere’s a simple example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-624860-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-624860-0\&quot;\u003elet a = 5;\nlet b = 3;\n\nconsole.log(a \u0026gt; b); // This will output: true\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this example, since \u003ccode\u003e5\u003c/code\u003e is greater than \u003ccode\u003e3\u003c/code\u003e, it returns \u003ccode\u003etrue\u003c/code\u003e. If you have a different question, feel free to ask!\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624860/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624860/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;In JavaScript, the greater sign `\u003e` is used as a comparison operator to check if the value on the left side is greater than the value on the right side. It returns `true` if the left value is greater, and `false` otherwise.\n\nHere’s a simple example:\n\n```javascript\nlet a = 5;\nlet b = 3;\n\nconsole.log(a \u003e b); // This will output: true\n```\n\nIn this example, since `5` is greater than `3`, it returns `true`. If you have a different question, feel free to ask!&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;what is greater sign in javascript?&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:2.1,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 2.1 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>In JavaScript, the greater sign <code>&gt;</code> is used as a comparison operator to check if the value on the left side is greater than the value on the right side. It returns <code>true</code> if the left value is greater, and <code>false</code> otherwise.</p>

<p>Here’s a simple example:</p>

<pre><header><div>javascript</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-624860-0">Copy code</div>
