  <div class="bg-secondary">
 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:624858,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-624858&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eIn JavaScript, the greater than sign (\u003ccode\u003e\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e) is a comparison operator used to compare two values. It evaluates to \u003ccode\u003etrue\u003c/code\u003e if the value on the left is greater than the value on the right; otherwise, it evaluates to \u003ccode\u003efalse\u003c/code\u003e. Here's how to use it in different scenarios:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-624858-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-624858-0\&quot;\u003e// Example 1: Comparing numbers\nlet a = 10;\nlet b = 5;\n\nif (a \u0026gt; b) {\n    console.log(`${a} is greater than ${b}`); // This will print: 10 is greater than 5\n}\n\n// Example 2: Comparing strings\nlet str1 = \&quot;apple\&quot;;\nlet str2 = \&quot;banana\&quot;;\n\nif (str1 \u0026gt; str2) {\n    console.log(`${str1} is greater than ${str2}`); // This won't print because \&quot;apple\&quot; is not greater than \&quot;banana\&quot; in lexicographical order\n} else {\n    console.log(`${str1} is not greater than ${str2}`); // This will print: apple is not greater than banana\n}\n\n// Example 3: Using in a function\nfunction isGreater(num1, num2) {\n    return num1 \u0026gt; num2;\n}\n\nconsole.log(isGreater(7, 4)); // This will print: true\nconsole.log(isGreater(2, 3)); // This will print: false\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the greater than operator in conditional statements, loops, or any expression that requires comparing values.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624858/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624858/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;In JavaScript, the greater than sign (`\u003e`) is a comparison operator used to compare two values. It evaluates to `true` if the value on the left is greater than the value on the right; otherwise, it evaluates to `false`. Here's how to use it in different scenarios:\n\n```javascript\n// Example 1: Comparing numbers\nlet a = 10;\nlet b = 5;\n\nif (a \u003e b) {\n    console.log(`${a} is greater than ${b}`); // This will print: 10 is greater than 5\n}\n\n// Example 2: Comparing strings\nlet str1 = \&quot;apple\&quot;;\nlet str2 = \&quot;banana\&quot;;\n\nif (str1 \u003e str2) {\n    console.log(`${str1} is greater than ${str2}`); // This won't print because \&quot;apple\&quot; is not greater than \&quot;banana\&quot; in lexicographical order\n} else {\n    console.log(`${str1} is not greater than ${str2}`); // This will print: apple is not greater than banana\n}\n\n// Example 3: Using in a function\nfunction isGreater(num1, num2) {\n    return num1 \u003e num2;\n}\n\nconsole.log(isGreater(7, 4)); // This will print: true\nconsole.log(isGreater(2, 3)); // This will print: false\n```\n\nYou can use the greater than operator in conditional statements, loops, or any expression that requires comparing values.&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;greater than sign in javascript and how to use it\r\n&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;code&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:7.12,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;code&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 7.12 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>In JavaScript, the greater than sign (<code>&gt;</code>) is a comparison operator used to compare two values. It evaluates to <code>true</code> if the value on the left is greater than the value on the right; otherwise, it evaluates to <code>false</code>. Here's how to use it in different scenarios:</p>

<pre><header><div>javascript</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-624858-0">Copy code</div>
</div></header><code class="javascript hljs language-javascript" id="code-624858-0" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment">// Example 1: Comparing numbers</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> a = <span class="hljs-number">10</span>;
