  <pre style="white-space: pre-wrap; " class="mb-0 text-dark"><p>what's the difference between git fetch and git pull?</p></pre>
</div>

  <div class="bg-secondary">
 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:624776,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-624776&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eGreat question! \u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003egit fetch\u003c/code\u003e is a command that downloads changes from a remote repository to your local repository, but it doesn't merge those changes into your working files. It basically updates your remote tracking branches to match the latest updates from the remote.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003egit pull\u003c/code\u003e is a command that does two actions: it first fetches the updates from the remote repository (like \u003ccode\u003egit fetch\u003c/code\u003e), and then it merges those changes into your current branch. So it actually updates your working files to include the latest changes as well.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere's a simple way to remember:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ebash\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-624776-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;bash\&quot; id=\&quot;code-624776-0\&quot;\u003e# Fetches changes from the remote\ngit fetch\n\n# Fetches and merges changes from the remote\ngit pull\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can think of \u003ccode\u003egit fetch\u003c/code\u003e as checking for mail, while \u003ccode\u003egit pull\u003c/code\u003e is retrieving the mail and putting it in your inbox!\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624776/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624776/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:624793,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-624793&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eRemote tracking branches are references in your local Git repository that keep track of the state of branches in a remote repository. They are basically pointers that show the last commit made on a branch in the remote repository.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you perform a \u003ccode\u003egit fetch\u003c/code\u003e, Git updates these remote tracking branches to reflect the latest changes in the remote repository. They are prefixed with the name of the remote, usually \u003ccode\u003eorigin\u003c/code\u003e, followed by a slash and the branch name. For example, \u003ccode\u003eorigin/main\u003c/code\u003e would represent the \u003ccode\u003emain\u003c/code\u003e branch on the remote named \u003ccode\u003eorigin\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere's a quick example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ebash\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-624793-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;bash\&quot; id=\&quot;code-624793-0\&quot;\u003e# Check the status of remote tracking branches\ngit branch -r\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis command will show you all the remote tracking branches. If you fetch updates, those branches will update to show the latest commits from the remote repository without affecting your local branches.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624793/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624793/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:624776,&quot;children&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:624800,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-624800&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eSheCodes Athena AI only answers technical questions.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624800/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624800/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:624793,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;SheCodes Athena AI only answers technical questions.&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;please could you explain what remote tracking branches are in more detail&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;follow_up&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:0.81,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;}],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;Remote tracking branches are references in your local Git repository that keep track of the state of branches in a remote repository. They are basically pointers that show the last commit made on a branch in the remote repository.\n\nWhen you perform a `git fetch`, Git updates these remote tracking branches to reflect the latest changes in the remote repository. They are prefixed with the name of the remote, usually `origin`, followed by a slash and the branch name. For example, `origin/main` would represent the `main` branch on the remote named `origin`.\n\nHere's a quick example:\n\n```bash\n# Check the status of remote tracking branches\ngit branch -r\n```\n\nThis command will show you all the remote tracking branches. If you fetch updates, those branches will update to show the latest commits from the remote repository without affecting your local branches.&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;what do you mean by remote tracking branches?&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;follow_up&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:3.48,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;}],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;Great question! \n\n- `git fetch` is a command that downloads changes from a remote repository to your local repository, but it doesn't merge those changes into your working files. It basically updates your remote tracking branches to match the latest updates from the remote.\n\n- `git pull` is a command that does two actions: it first fetches the updates from the remote repository (like `git fetch`), and then it merges those changes into your current branch. So it actually updates your working files to include the latest changes as well.\n\nHere's a simple way to remember:\n\n```bash\n# Fetches changes from the remote\ngit fetch\n\n# Fetches and merges changes from the remote\ngit pull\n```\n\nYou can think of `git fetch` as checking for mail, while `git pull` is retrieving the mail and putting it in your inbox!&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;what's the difference between git fetch and git pull?&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:3.64,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 3.64 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>Great question! </p>

<ul>
<li><p><code>git fetch</code> is a command that downloads changes from a remote repository to your local repository, but it doesn't merge those changes into your working files. It basically updates your remote tracking branches to match the latest updates from the remote.</p></li>
<li><p><code>git pull</code> is a command that does two actions: it first fetches the updates from the remote repository (like <code>git fetch</code>), and then it merges those changes into your current branch. So it actually updates your working files to include the latest changes as well.</p></li>
</ul>

<p>Here's a simple way to remember:</p>

<pre><header><div>bash</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-624776-0">Copy code</div>
</div></header><code class="bash hljs language-bash" id="code-624776-0" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment"># Fetches changes from the remote</span>
git fetch

<span class="hljs-comment"># Fetches and merges changes from the remote</span>
git pull
</code></pre>

<p>You can think of <code>git fetch</code> as checking for mail, while <code>git pull</code> is retrieving the mail and putting it in your inbox!</p>
</div></div><div class="athena-follow-ups"><div><div id="question-624793" class="athena-request athena-request-question"><p>👩‍💻 what do you mean by remote tracking branches?</p></div><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 3.48 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>Remote tracking branches are references in your local Git repository that keep track of the state of branches in a remote repository. They are basically pointers that show the last commit made on a branch in the remote repository.</p>

<p>When you perform a <code>git fetch</code>, Git updates these remote tracking branches to reflect the latest changes in the remote repository. They are prefixed with the name of the remote, usually <code>origin</code>, followed by a slash and the branch name. For example, <code>origin/main</code> would represent the <code>main</code> branch on the remote named <code>origin</code>.</p>

<p>Here's a quick example:</p>

<pre><header><div>bash</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-624793-0">Copy code</div>
</div></header><code class="bash hljs language-bash" id="code-624793-0" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment"># Check the status of remote tracking branches</span>
git branch -r
</code></pre>

<p>This command will show you all the remote tracking branches. If you fetch updates, those branches will update to show the latest commits from the remote repository without affecting your local branches.</p>
</div></div><div class="athena-follow-ups"><div><div id="question-624800" class="athena-request athena-request-question"><p>👩‍💻 please could you explain what remote tracking branches are in more detail</p></div><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 0.81 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>SheCodes Athena AI only answers technical questions.</p>
</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>
