  <pre style="white-space: pre-wrap; " class="mb-0 text-dark"><p>What does textcontent mean in javascript</p></pre>
</div>

  <div class="bg-secondary">
 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:624614,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-624614&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eIn JavaScript, \u003ccode\u003etextContent\u003c/code\u003e is a property that allows you to get or set the text inside an HTML element. It includes all the text, but it ignores any HTML tags within that element.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere's an example of how to use \u003ccode\u003etextContent\u003c/code\u003e:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-624614-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-624614-0\&quot;\u003e// Assume you have an HTML element like this:\n// \u0026lt;div id=\&quot;myElement\&quot;\u0026gt;Hello, \u0026lt;strong\u0026gt;World!\u0026lt;/strong\u0026gt;\u0026lt;/div\u0026gt;\n\n// Get the text content of the element\nlet element = document.getElementById('myElement');\nconsole.log(element.textContent); // This will log: \&quot;Hello, World!\&quot;\n\n// Set new text content\nelement.textContent = 'New text!';\nconsole.log(element.textContent); // This will log: \&quot;New text!\&quot;\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can read more about it on \u003ca href=\&quot;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Node/textContent\&quot; target=\&quot;_blank\&quot;\u003eMDN Web Docs - Element.textContent\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624614/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/624614/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;In JavaScript, `textContent` is a property that allows you to get or set the text inside an HTML element. It includes all the text, but it ignores any HTML tags within that element.\n\nHere's an example of how to use `textContent`:\n\n```javascript\n// Assume you have an HTML element like this:\n// \u003cdiv id=\&quot;myElement\&quot;\u003eHello, \u003cstrong\u003eWorld!\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\n// Get the text content of the element\nlet element = document.getElementById('myElement');\nconsole.log(element.textContent); // This will log: \&quot;Hello, World!\&quot;\n\n// Set new text content\nelement.textContent = 'New text!';\nconsole.log(element.textContent); // This will log: \&quot;New text!\&quot;\n```\n\nYou can read more about it on [MDN Web Docs - Element.textContent](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Node/textContent).&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;What does textcontent mean in javascript\r\n&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:2.84,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 2.84 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>In JavaScript, <code>textContent</code> is a property that allows you to get or set the text inside an HTML element. It includes all the text, but it ignores any HTML tags within that element.</p>

<p>Here's an example of how to use <code>textContent</code>:</p>

<pre><header><div>javascript</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-624614-0">Copy code</div>
</div></header><code class="javascript hljs language-javascript" id="code-624614-0" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-comment">// Assume you have an HTML element like this:</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">// &lt;div id="myElement"&gt;Hello, &lt;strong&gt;World!&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Get the text content of the element</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> element = <span class="hljs-variable language_">document</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">getElementById</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'myElement'</span>);
<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(element.<span class="hljs-property">textContent</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// This will log: "Hello, World!"</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Set new text content</span>
element.<span class="hljs-property">textContent</span> = <span class="hljs-string">'New text!'</span>;
<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(element.<span class="hljs-property">textContent</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// This will log: "New text!"</span>
</code></pre>

<p>You can read more about it on <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Node/textContent" target="_blank">MDN Web Docs - Element.textContent</a>.</p>
</div></div></div></div>
