  <pre style="white-space: pre-wrap; " class="mb-0 text-dark"><p>What is the logical AND operator in JavaScript?</p></pre>
</div>

  <div class="bg-secondary">
 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:610885,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-610885&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eThe logical AND operator in JavaScript is represented by \u003ccode\u003e\u0026amp;\u0026amp;\u003c/code\u003e. It is used to combine two or more conditions and returns \u003ccode\u003etrue\u003c/code\u003e only if all conditions are true.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere’s a simple example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-610885-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-610885-0\&quot;\u003elet a = true;\nlet b = false;\n\nconsole.log(a \u0026amp;\u0026amp; b); // Output: false\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this example, \u003ccode\u003ea\u003c/code\u003e is \u003ccode\u003etrue\u003c/code\u003e, and \u003ccode\u003eb\u003c/code\u003e is \u003ccode\u003efalse\u003c/code\u003e. The expression \u003ccode\u003ea \u0026amp;\u0026amp; b\u003c/code\u003e evaluates to \u003ccode\u003efalse\u003c/code\u003e because both conditions need to be true for the result to be true.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can learn more about it in the \u003ca href=\&quot;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Logical_AND\&quot; target=\&quot;_blank\&quot;\u003eMDN web docs\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/610885/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/610885/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;The logical AND operator in JavaScript is represented by `\u0026\u0026`. It is used to combine two or more conditions and returns `true` only if all conditions are true.\n\nHere’s a simple example:\n\n```javascript\nlet a = true;\nlet b = false;\n\nconsole.log(a \u0026\u0026 b); // Output: false\n```\n\nIn this example, `a` is `true`, and `b` is `false`. The expression `a \u0026\u0026 b` evaluates to `false` because both conditions need to be true for the result to be true.\n\nYou can learn more about it in the [MDN web docs](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Logical_AND).&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;What is the logical AND operator in JavaScript?&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:2.49,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 2.49 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>The logical AND operator in JavaScript is represented by <code>&amp;&amp;</code>. It is used to combine two or more conditions and returns <code>true</code> only if all conditions are true.</p>

<p>Here’s a simple example:</p>

<pre><header><div>javascript</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-610885-0">Copy code</div>
</div></header><code class="javascript hljs language-javascript" id="code-610885-0" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> a = <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> b = <span class="hljs-literal">false</span>;

<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(a &amp;&amp; b); <span class="hljs-comment">// Output: false</span>
</code></pre>

<p>In this example, <code>a</code> is <code>true</code>, and <code>b</code> is <code>false</code>. The expression <code>a &amp;&amp; b</code> evaluates to <code>false</code> because both conditions need to be true for the result to be true.</p>

<p>You can learn more about it in the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Logical_AND" target="_blank">MDN web docs</a>.</p>
</div></div></div></div>
