 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:605520,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-605520&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eIn JavaScript, a break statement is used to stop the execution of a loop or switch statement. When the break statement is reached, the program will exit the loop or switch and continue with the next line of code after it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere’s a simple example with a loop:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-605520-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-605520-0\&quot;\u003efor (let i = 0; i \u0026lt; 10; i++) {\n    if (i === 5) {\n        break; // Exit the loop when i is 5\n    }\n    console.log(i);\n}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this example, the loop prints numbers from 0 to 4. When \u003ccode\u003ei\u003c/code\u003e becomes 5, the break statement is executed, and the loop ends.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can learn more about break statements in JavaScript on \u003ca href=\&quot;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/break\&quot; target=\&quot;_blank\&quot;\u003eMDN Web Docs\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/605520/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/605520/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;In JavaScript, a break statement is used to stop the execution of a loop or switch statement. When the break statement is reached, the program will exit the loop or switch and continue with the next line of code after it.\n\nHere’s a simple example with a loop:\n\n```javascript\nfor (let i = 0; i \u003c 10; i++) {\n    if (i === 5) {\n        break; // Exit the loop when i is 5\n    }\n    console.log(i);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the loop prints numbers from 0 to 4. When `i` becomes 5, the break statement is executed, and the loop ends.\n\nYou can learn more about break statements in JavaScript on [MDN Web Docs](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/break).&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;what are break statements in javascript\r\n&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:3.23,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 3.23 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>In JavaScript, a break statement is used to stop the execution of a loop or switch statement. When the break statement is reached, the program will exit the loop or switch and continue with the next line of code after it.</p>

<p>Here’s a simple example with a loop:</p>

<pre><header><div>javascript</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-605520-0">Copy code</div>
</div></header><code class="javascript hljs language-javascript" id="code-605520-0" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">for</span> (<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> i = <span class="hljs-number">0</span>; i &lt; <span class="hljs-number">10</span>; i++) {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (i === <span class="hljs-number">5</span>) {
        <span class="hljs-keyword">break</span>; <span class="hljs-comment">// Exit the loop when i is 5</span>
    }
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(i);
}
</code></pre>

<p>In this example, the loop prints numbers from 0 to 4. When <code>i</code> becomes 5, the break statement is executed, and the loop ends.</p>

<p>You can learn more about break statements in JavaScript on <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/break" target="_blank">MDN Web Docs</a>.</p>
