 <div data-react-class="AthenaRequestAnswer" data-react-props="{&quot;request&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:581484,&quot;broadcast_channel&quot;:&quot;athena-request-581484&quot;,&quot;answer_as_markdown&quot;:&quot;\u003cp\u003eIn JavaScript, the \u003ccode\u003etrim()\u003c/code\u003e method is used to remove whitespace from both ends of a string. This is useful when you want to clean up user input.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere's how you can use it:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003cheader\u003e\u003cdiv\u003ejavascript\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\&quot;d-flex\&quot;\u003e \u003ci class=\&quot;fas fa-copy\&quot;\u003e\u003c/i\u003e\u003cdiv class=\&quot;clipboard-with-label-confirmation\&quot; data-clipboard-target=\&quot;#code-581484-0\&quot;\u003eCopy code\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/header\u003e\u003ccode class=\&quot;javascript\&quot; id=\&quot;code-581484-0\&quot;\u003elet myString = \&quot;   Hello, World!   \&quot;;\nlet trimmedString = myString.trim();\n\nconsole.log(trimmedString); // \&quot;Hello, World!\&quot;\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this example, \u003ccode\u003etrim()\u003c/code\u003e removes the spaces before \&quot;Hello,\&quot; and after \&quot;World!\&quot; and gives us a clean string. \u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can find more information about string methods in JavaScript \u003ca href=\&quot;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/trim\&quot; target=\&quot;_blank\&quot;\u003ehere\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n&quot;,&quot;downvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/581484/downvote&quot;,&quot;upvote_path&quot;:&quot;/athena_requests/581484/upvote&quot;,&quot;upvoted&quot;:false,&quot;downvoted&quot;:false,&quot;parent_id&quot;:null,&quot;children&quot;:[],&quot;state&quot;:&quot;processed&quot;,&quot;answer&quot;:&quot;In JavaScript, the `trim()` method is used to remove whitespace from both ends of a string. This is useful when you want to clean up user input.\n\nHere's how you can use it:\n\n```javascript\nlet myString = \&quot;   Hello, World!   \&quot;;\nlet trimmedString = myString.trim();\n\nconsole.log(trimmedString); // \&quot;Hello, World!\&quot;\n```\n\nIn this example, `trim()` removes the spaces before \&quot;Hello,\&quot; and after \&quot;World!\&quot; and gives us a clean string. \n\nYou can find more information about string methods in JavaScript [here](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/trim).&quot;,&quot;question&quot;:&quot;how to use trim in js\r\n&quot;,&quot;question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;,&quot;question_format&quot;:&quot;text&quot;,&quot;processing_time&quot;:7.27,&quot;root_question_type&quot;:&quot;question&quot;},&quot;hideStatus&quot;:true}" data-react-cache-id="AthenaRequestAnswer-0"><div><div class="athena-request athena-request-answer"><div class="d-flex justify-content-between"><h3>🤖 SheCodes Athena says:</h3><div class="d-flex"><div class="text-muted d-none d-lg-block">Answered in 7.27 seconds</div></div></div><div><p>In JavaScript, the <code>trim()</code> method is used to remove whitespace from both ends of a string. This is useful when you want to clean up user input.</p>

<p>Here's how you can use it:</p>

<pre><header><div>javascript</div> <div class="d-flex"> <i class="fas fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i><div class="clipboard-with-label-confirmation" data-clipboard-target="#code-581484-0">Copy code</div>
</div></header><code class="javascript hljs language-javascript" id="code-581484-0" data-highlighted="yes"><span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> myString = <span class="hljs-string">"   Hello, World!   "</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> trimmedString = myString.<span class="hljs-title function_">trim</span>();

<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(trimmedString); <span class="hljs-comment">// "Hello, World!"</span>
</code></pre>

<p>In this example, <code>trim()</code> removes the spaces before "Hello," and after "World!" and gives us a clean string. </p>

<p>You can find more information about string methods in JavaScript <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/trim" target="_blank">here</a>.</p>
